# RegulAIt — the Cloudflare Tunnel front (ADR-0203 lane T).
#
# The laptop-hosted demo has no inbound port. Cloudflare terminates TLS at its
# edge for the public name, and `cloudflared` (compose profile `tunnel`) holds
# an OUTBOUND connection to Cloudflare and hands each request to THIS Caddy as
# plain HTTP over a private compose network (`tunnel`) that only the two of
# them are on. From here on the request takes the same routes as the TLS front
# (./routes.Caddyfile): the /idp allow-list, the gateway proxy, the overwritten
# forwarding headers.
#
#   internet --HTTPS--> Cloudflare edge <--outbound tunnel-- cloudflared
#        --HTTP, `tunnel` network--> this Caddy (172.28.0.2 on `regulait`)
#        --> gateway:3000 / keycloak:8080
#
# This file never asks for a certificate: there is no ACME, no :80, no :443, no
# published port. Caddy listens on ONE address, its own on the `tunnel`
# network, so nothing on the `regulait` network (db, object store, Keycloak,
# the gateway itself) can reach this listener at all.
#
# Who may speak for the client, and how that is checked:
#
#   * The peer must be the cloudflared container's fixed address. Anything else
#     is dropped before any header is read. That is what makes it safe to read
#     CF-Connecting-IP below: Cloudflare's edge sets that header itself
#     (overwriting a client-sent one), and only cloudflared can reach here.
#   * CF-Connecting-IP must be present and look like one IP address, or the
#     request is refused (400). It becomes X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP; the
#     header itself is then stripped (routes.Caddyfile), so nothing behind
#     Caddy ever reads it. Any client-sent X-Forwarded-For is discarded.
#   * Cloudflare's edge sets X-Forwarded-Proto to the scheme the browser used.
#     Anything but exactly `https` is redirected to https:// before proxying,
#     so the `https` this front then sends upstream is true. (Turn on "Always
#     Use HTTPS" for the zone too: docs/deployment/TUNNEL.md.)
#   * The site only answers for REGULAIT_PUBLIC_HOST; another Host header gets
#     no route.
#
# The gateway and Keycloak keep trusting X-Forwarded-* from 172.28.0.2 only
# (ADR-0031). This front holds that address on the `regulait` network, exactly
# like the TLS front, which is why the `tunnel` and `hosted`/`tls` profiles are
# mutually exclusive: start both and compose refuses the second container
# ("Address already in use"). One public door at a time.
#
#   REGULAIT_PUBLIC_HOST             the public name (REQUIRED; the default is a
#                                    `.invalid` placeholder that matches no real
#                                    request, so an unset host serves nothing)
#   REGULAIT_TUNNEL_CADDY_ADDR       this listener's address  (compose sets it)
#   REGULAIT_TUNNEL_CLOUDFLARED_ADDR the only peer accepted   (compose sets it)
{
	# No certificates, no HTTP->HTTPS redirect server, no ACME: TLS ends at
	# Cloudflare's edge.
	auto_https off
}

import routes.Caddyfile

http://{$REGULAIT_PUBLIC_HOST:set-regulait-public-host.invalid}:8080 {
	bind {$REGULAIT_TUNNEL_CADDY_ADDR:127.0.0.1}

	@not_cloudflared not remote_ip {$REGULAIT_TUNNEL_CLOUDFLARED_ADDR:127.0.0.1}/32
	# Matched on the placeholder, not with the `header` matcher: a placeholder
	# joins repeated header lines with a comma, so a second X-Forwarded-Proto or
	# CF-Connecting-IP line fails the anchored pattern instead of slipping past
	# a matcher that accepts any one matching line.
	@not_https not vars_regexp {http.request.header.X-Forwarded-Proto} ^https$
	# one IPv4 or IPv6 address, nothing else (no list, no port, no name)
	@bad_client_ip not vars_regexp {http.request.header.CF-Connecting-IP} ^[0-9A-Fa-f:.]{2,45}$

	import regulait_headers

	# `route` keeps these in the written order: the peer check first, then the
	# scheme, then the client address, and only then the shared routes.
	route {
		abort @not_cloudflared
		redir @not_https https://{host}{uri} 308
		respond @bad_client_ip "missing or malformed CF-Connecting-IP" 400
		import regulait_routes {http.request.header.CF-Connecting-IP} https
	}

	# Access log to stdout, so `docker compose logs caddy-tunnel` is the one place to look.
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
